─── tests/test_every_claimed_suffix_is_supported.py:282-290 ───
[maintainability · medium] The docstring promises "a run that dies mid-encode leaves nothing for the
next one to copy as a sample", but this only holds for the cached-name file under `_SAMPLES`. If
`write(building)` raises mid-encode, neither `os.replace` nor the `contextlib.suppress(OSError)
scratch.rmdir()` runs, so the partial `building-*` artifact persists inside the scratch directory
and prevents the scratch dir from ever being removed. Over many failed runs the scratch dir
accumulates orphan building files. Consider wrapping `write(building)` and `os.replace` in a
try/finally so the partial file (and the scratch dir) is cleaned up on failure, or moving the
cleanup out of the `os.replace` branch.



─── tests/test_every_claimed_suffix_is_supported.py:295-297 ───
[maintainability · medium] Importing this test module now has the side effect of creating
`.pytest_cache/suffix-samples/` under the repo root (`_SAMPLES.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)`).
In environments where the source directory is read-only or where the test runner lacks write
permission (CI containers, sandboxes, installed copies), test collection itself will fail with
`PermissionError` instead of failing inside a specific test. The pre-PR module had no import-time
filesystem side effects. Consider deferring the directory creation to the first call inside
`_cached` so importing the module remains pure.



─── tests/test_every_claimed_suffix_is_supported.py:460-462 ───
[style · low] `developed.copy()` here is redundant. `oriented.upright` returns
`image.transpose(turn)` (a new Image; PIL `transpose` does not mutate), and `decode.open_still`
already returns an in-memory image for RAW (`Image.fromarray(array)`), so there is no lazy handle or
shared buffer to defensively copy. Passing `developed` directly is equivalent and avoids an extra
full-frame memcpy of a ~22-megapixel development.



